			Content >>
				The walnut for this collection comes from trees felled in winter, when the sap is low, and dried in the
				open air for two years before it reaches the workshop. Each board is then planed by hand and checked
				for knots and cracks. Only the boards that pass go to the cutting table, where the parts of each chair
				are marked with a template. The joints are cut with chisels, glued with hide glue and pressed for a
				day. After a light sanding, the wood takes three coats of linseed oil, one each week, and a last coat
				of beeswax. The seat is woven from natural wool on a frame of ash. The whole process takes six weeks,
				and every chair carries the initials of the person who made it under the seat.
